      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Electoral College indeed raises significant questions about the principle of \"one person, one vote,\" and whether it remains a suitable framework for modern American democracy. Proponents argue that the system maintains a balance of power and safeguards against electoral tyranny, potentially preventing candidates from solely focusing on high-population areas. It also necessitates coalition-building across diverse regions, ensuring broader representation. Nonetheless, critics claim it results in a presidency not directly tied to the majority's preference, provoking debates about democracy in action. Ensuring the Electoral College aligns with today's societal values is an ongoing discussion, with various reform options on the table, including the adoption of a direct popular vote system. This proposal has its champions and detractors, who argue about its chances of ratification and the risks of the incumbent party maintaining power under a new rule without major disruptive elections. Ultimately, each system comes with trade-offs, underscoring the complexity inherent in balancing democratic ideals with pragmatic governance.",
